 "final_text": "The submitted edit raises practice worst-group accuracy from 72.9% to 91.0% and keeps the weighted mean at 96.6% (was 97.7%). All ten forward passes are used, and `work/edited` holds exactly the tensors measured in the last pass. Only `fc.weight` and `fc.bias` are changed.\n\n## What I changed\n\nWriting `d = w1 \u2212 w0` for the decision direction of the last layer:\n\n- **New direction:** `d' = d \u2212 (d\u00b7D)\u00b7v`, split symmetrically over the two rows (`w1 += \u03b4/2`, `w0 \u2212= \u03b4/2`, so `w0 + w1` is unchanged).\n- **`D`** is the background direction in the 2,048 pooled features: the mean of water \u2212 land, taken within each bird class.\n- **`v`** is a scene detector: `D` with its component along the bird direction removed, scaled so that `v\u00b7D = 1`.\n- **Bias:** the removed coordinate is re-centred at the group-balanced mean, so the margin of an \"average bird on a neutral scene\" is unchanged. Biases go from [0.026, 0.011] to [\u22123.151, 3.187].\n\nThis is the smallest change of the read-out that makes its mean response to the background zero while leaving its response to the bird direction exactly as it was. It is a rank-one change of norm 1.42, built only from group means and the existing weights; nothing was fitted to labels.\n\n## Result on the practice images (final pass, paired with the original)\n\n| | landbird/land | landbird/water | waterbird/land | waterbird/water | worst | weighted mean |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| original | 99.36 | 82.62 | 72.93 | 96.24 | 72.93 | 97.72 |\n| edited | 98.07 | 93.13 | 90.98 | 92.48 | **90.98** | **96.57** |\n| wrong\u2192right / right\u2192wrong | 0 / 6 | 51 / 2 | 24 / 0 | 0 / 5 | | |\n\nStandard errors of the edited group accuracies are 0.6, 1.2, 2.5 and 2.3 points, so the minority-group gains are many standard errors and the majority-group losses are small.\n\n## How I got there\n\n1. **Diagnosis.** The background moves the original margin by +8.8 logits for landbirds and +7.4 for waterbirds; the bird moves it by about 16. Bird-free background images are called \"waterbird\" 78% of the time on water scenes and 3% on land scenes.\n2. **Where it enters.** In `layer3` the background and bird directions are orthogonal (cosine 0.04); by the output of the last block they are entangled (0.57). Read through `fc`, the skip path carries about 3.3 logits of background, and the residual branch of `layer4.2` adds about 4.7 logits of background and 13.9 of bird evidence.\n3. **Causal tests upstream.** Cutting one scene direction at the input of `layer4.2` (a 3% change of `conv1`, original `fc`) shrank the background gap from 8.4/7.7 to 2.1/4.5 logits, as predicted. A second cut to the \"land\" value met three predictions written beforehand: same residual gap, water images pulled down by 6.3 and 3.0 logits, land images unchanged. I did not use this cut in the submission: the block's response is saturating, so the result depends strongly on the reference value, and it separates birds no better than the `fc` edit.\n4. **How `fc` combines evidence.** About 84% of `d` lies in the plane of the background and bird directions. Removing the bird component instead of the scene component turns the read-out into a scene classifier (agrees with the background 91%, with the bird 49%); the submitted edit does the opposite (52% / 94%).\n5. **Dose-response and control.** At removal strengths 0, 0.5, 1 and 1.5:\n   - the background gap for landbirds is +8.8, +4.4, +0.1 and \u22124.3 logits;\n   - worst-group accuracy is 72.9, 86.5, 91.0 and 89.5;\n   - at 1.5 the weighted mean falls to 93.4.\n\n   An equal-sized change along a random direction does nothing (99.4 / 82.4 / 71.4 / 96.2).\n6. **Held-out checks.**\n   - Cross-fitting (each image scored by the edit built from the other half) gives 98.3 / 92.7 / 91.0 / 92.5, the same as in-sample.\n   - On composites with textures never used to build anything, swapping land for water texture behind a fixed bird moves the original margin by +8.5 \u00b1 0.3 logits and the edited one by \u22120.3 \u00b1 0.2.\n   - Horizontally flipped waterbird images score 90.2% and 94.3%.\n\n## What to expect on the evaluation\n\nI expect a worst group of about 90\u201391% and a mean of about 96.5%. The mean target is safe. The worst-group target is not certain: the two waterbird groups have only 133 practice images each, and a rough simulation from the practice counts puts the chance of meeting both targets at around 80%.\n\n- **Ceiling.** The remaining errors are mostly not background-related: landbird-looking species that carry the waterbird label (flycatcher-like birds, towhees), tiny or nearly invisible birds, and water photos that contain real waterbirds. That caps the waterbird groups at roughly 93%.\n- **Class-prior removal, not included.** Adding log(0.768/0.232) = 1.20 to the waterbird logit gave 96.4 / 90.8 / 93.2 / 93.2 with mean 95.4. Its gain (4 waterbirds fixed) is within noise, it broke 19 landbirds, and its nominal edge in pass probability (0.84 vs 0.79) vanished under a half-point pessimistic shift. A selection rule I fixed beforehand chose the unchanged bias.\n- **Bird-free scenes.** On background-only images the land/water margin gap falls from 10.9 to about 2.3 logits, not to zero.\n\nOne thing to know about method: because `fc` is linear with two output rows, in the exploratory passes I put one candidate read-out in each row and read the two logits separately, so each such pass measured two candidates. The tool's own accuracy summary is meaningless for those passes; the final pass used the real tensors.\n\nNotes are in `work/notes.md` and a copy of the final tensors in `work/best_final/`."
